examples.question_text:
Sketch $\operatorname{cosec}x$ for $0\le x\le2\pi$.
Label the asymptotes and state the period.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (0,0)--(6.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-1.5)--(0,1.6) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \a/\b in {1.5708/{\pi/2},3.1416/{\pi},4.7124/{3\pi/2},6.2832/{2\pi}} {\draw[black] (\a,-0.05)--(\a,0.05);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,0) {$\b$};}
\foreach \a in {-1,1} {\draw[black] (-0.05,\a)--(0.05,\a);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,\a) {$\a$};}
\draw[thick,curveBlue,domain=0:6.283185,samples=121] plot (\x,{sin(\x r)});
\node[text=curveBlue] at (3.2,1.4) {$y=\sin x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Use $\operatorname{cosec}x=1/\sin x$. Sine is zero at $x=0,\pi,2\pi$, giving vertical asymptotes there.
The points $(\pi/2,1)$ and $(3\pi/2,-1)$ retain their ordinates when reciprocated. Draw a positive branch on $(0,\pi)$ and a negative branch on $(\pi,2\pi)$, approaching the asymptotes.
The period is $2\pi$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (0,0)--(6.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-3.3)--(0,3.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \a/\b in {1.5708/{\pi/2},3.1416/{\pi},4.7124/{3\pi/2},6.2832/{2\pi}} {\draw[black] (\a,-0.06)--(\a,0.06);\node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,0) {$\b$};}
\foreach \a in {-1,1} {\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,\a) {$\a$};}
\foreach \a in {0,3.141593,6.283185} {\draw[black,dashed] (\a,-3.1)--(\a,3.1);}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=0.339837:2.801756,samples=121] plot (\x,{1/sin(\x r)});
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=3.481430:5.943348,samples=121] plot (\x,{1/sin(\x r)});
\fill[curveBlue] (1.570796,1) circle (1pt);
\fill[curveBlue] (4.712389,-1) circle (1pt);
\node[above,text=curveBlue] at (1.570796,1) {$(\pi/2,1)$};
\node[below,text=curveBlue] at (4.712389,-1) {$(3\pi/2,-1)$};
\node[text=curveBlue] at (4.8,2.3) {$y=\operatorname{cosec}x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Reciprocate each non-zero cosine ordinate; a zero cosine ordinate gives an undefined secant value.
$\begin{array}{c|ccccc}x&-\pi&-\pi/2&0&\pi/2&\pi\\\hline\cos x&-1&0&1&0&-1\\\sec x&-1&\text{undefined}&1&\text{undefined}&-1\end{array}$
The vertical asymptotes are $x=-\pi/2$ and $x=\pi/2$. The central branch has minimum $(0,1)$; the outer branches are negative and include the endpoints $(-\pi,-1)$ and $(\pi,-1)$.
Domain: $[-\pi,-\pi/2)\cup(-\pi/2,\pi/2)\cup(\pi/2,\pi]$.
Range: $(-\infty,-1]\cup[1,\infty)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-3.4,0)--(3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-3.3)--(0,3.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \a/\b in {-3.141593/{-\pi},-1.570796/{-\pi/2},1.570796/{\pi/2},3.141593/{\pi}} {\draw[black] (\a,-0.06)--(\a,0.06);\node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,0) {$\b$};}
\foreach \a in {-1,1} {\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,\a) {$\a$};}
\foreach \a in {-1.570796,1.570796} {\draw[black,dashed] (\a,-3.1)--(\a,3.1);}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,->,domain=-3.141593:-1.910633,samples=81] plot (\x,{1/cos(\x r)});
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-1.230959:1.230959,samples=121] plot (\x,{1/cos(\x r)});
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<-,domain=1.910633:3.141593,samples=81] plot (\x,{1/cos(\x r)});
\foreach \a/\b in {-3.141593/-1,0/1,3.141593/-1} {\fill[curveBlue] (\a,\b) circle (1pt);}
\node[above right,text=curveBlue] at (0,1) {$(0,1)$};
\node[text=curveBlue] at (2.6,2.3) {$y=\sec x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Use $\cot x=\cos x/\sin x$. Cotangent is undefined where sine is zero, and is zero where cosine is zero and sine is non-zero.
$\begin{array}{c|ccccc}x&-\pi&-\pi/2&0&\pi/2&\pi\\\hline\cot x&\text{undefined}&0&\text{undefined}&0&\text{undefined}\end{array}$
The vertical asymptotes are $x=-\pi,0,\pi$. Each branch decreases from $+\infty$ to $-\infty$, crossing the axis at $(-\pi/2,0)$ or $(\pi/2,0)$.
Domain: $(-\pi,0)\cup(0,\pi)$. Range: $\mathbb R$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-3.4,0)--(3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-3.3)--(0,3.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \a/\b in {-3.141593/{-\pi},-1.570796/{-\pi/2},1.570796/{\pi/2},3.141593/{\pi}} {\draw[black] (\a,-0.06)--(\a,0.06);\node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,0) {$\b$};}
\foreach \a in {-3.141593,0,3.141593} {\draw[black,dashed] (\a,-3.1)--(\a,3.1);}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-2.819842:-0.321751,samples=121] plot (\x,{cos(\x r)/sin(\x r)});
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=0.321751:2.819842,samples=121] plot (\x,{cos(\x r)/sin(\x r)});
\fill[curveBlue] (-1.570796,0) circle (1pt);
\fill[curveBlue] (1.570796,0) circle (1pt);
\node[text=curveBlue] at (2.4,2.5) {$y=\cot x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
